From 097def7cde2cbcd5737df19c27d2c4be89d831f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 06:39:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0193 -- deferred MIDI port writes, replayed by the
 SB IRQ (opt-in)

WHY: dosags (PLAN.md, 8.3 re-run SB4O/SB4W, 2026-09-23) runs its MIDI scheduler (the hub's shared
midi_sched + opl3_sink / waveblaster_sink) from the engine's main loop. On the 486 the 640x480
intro renders at ~6 fps and the fixed-timestep loop runs its catch-up ticks back to back, so every
event due between two renders went out in one batch: 465 of 483 lateness records were regular
150-200 ms gaps, 72% of all events more than 50 ms late -- the operator heard it as the music
"speeding up randomly". Tempo overall was right (song clock vs RTC 0.997-1.003). doskutsu solved
the same problem with SDL/0072, running its own fixed-point scheduler inside the IRQ; dosags cannot:
the shared midi_sched_tick uses the FPU (event_us = (double)abs_tick * us_per_tick), the sinks
allocate and keep unlocked state, and that code is the hub's, not dosags'.

WHAT: let the port run its scheduler AHEAD on the main thread and have the SB ISR play the result on
time. SDL_DOSMidiDeferEnable(kind) (SDL_dosmidi_tick.h) turns on capture for one kind: 1 = OPL
register writes (SDL_DOSOpl3WriteRegister), 2 = MPU-401 bytes (SDL_DOSMpu401WriteByte), only on the
blind direct-port transport -- the SDL/0093 DRR status read (ISA IOCHRDY can stall the CPU), the
SDL/0098 TX-delay PIT busy-wait (the cold-init default, 320 us per byte) and the DSP-mediated path
are refused, as is a closed SB device. Captured writes go into a fixed 8192-entry queue stamped with
the current stamp (SDL_DOSMidiDeferSetStamp, an IRQ index; SDL_DOSMidiDeferIrqNow returns
dos_port_audio_irq_count, one IRQ = one DMA half-buffer, the device's own clock). On every IRQ, after
the existing counters, the ISR writes out the queue head while its stamp is due, at most 256 entries
per IRQ: raw outportb/inportb only, the same OPL address/data sequence as SDL_DOSOpl3WriteRegister
without its diagnostic uclock(); no FPU, no allocation, no SDL call. The drain code and the queue are
DPMI-locked at the first enable. Single producer (main thread) / single consumer (ISR): the entry is
stored, a compiler barrier, then the tail moves; the ISR reads the entry after seeing the tail.

Ordering and bounds:
- SDL_DOSOpl3Detect / InitChip / Shutdown HOLD the deferral while they run (SDL_DOSMidiDeferHold):
  their writes bypass the capture and the ISR writes nothing until they finish, so it cannot
  interleave with their address/data writes -- including across Detect's SDL_DelayPrecise, whose
  DOS_Yield thread switch ends in iret and re-enables interrupts (a first version relied on cli
  there, which did not hold). Queued entries just wait. With deferral off they run as before.
- Enable(0) stops the ISR consuming, then writes out at most 1024 queued entries from the main thread
  in batches of 32 with interrupts off only around each batch, and discards the rest, counted.
- CloseDevice with deferral on stops it (later writes go direct) and discards the queue, counted.
- SDL_DOSMidiDeferStats / Stats2: entries written, written >= 1 IRQ late, the most IRQs late, the
  most in one IRQ, captures refused (queue full), entries discarded, entries written by a disable.

Default off. For a port that never calls SDL_DOSMidiDeferEnable -- doskutsu, whose SDL/0072 ISR tick
is untouched -- the change is: one test of a locked byte per IRQ, and one test of two locked bytes
per OPL register write / MPU byte, including those its own 0072 ISR callback makes (g_mdq_kind and
g_mdq_hold are locked at OpenDevice with the ISR's other variables). Behaviour is otherwise identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
